  "page_id": "FI-28",
  "section_header": "EFI SYSTEM — Troubleshooting with Volt/Ohmmeter",
  "title": "TROUBLESHOOTING WITH VOLT/OHMMETER",
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "PREPARATION FOR TROUBLESHOOTING"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "Remove the glove box door and glove box.",
        "Remove the ECU with wire harness."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "index": 1,
      "description": "ECU wiring connector diagram showing voltmeter connection points. Shows ECU unit with connectors labeled B and E1, with a voltmeter connected to test terminals.",
      "labels": [
        "Voltmeter",
        "ECU",
        "B",
        "E1",
        "V"
      ],
      "bbox": {
        "top": 24,
        "left": 3,
        "width": 38,
        "height": 17
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "EFI SYSTEM CHECK PROCEDURE"
    },
    {
      "type": "notice",
      "text": "NOTE:\n1. The EFI circuit can be checked by measuring the resistance and voltage at the wiring connectors of the ECU.\n2. Perform all voltage measurement with the connectors connected.\n3. Verify that the battery voltage is 11V or above when the ignition switch is ON."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Using a voltmeter, measure the voltage at each terminal of the wiring connector."
    },
    {
      "type": "notice",
      "text": "NOTE: If there is any problem, see TROUBLESHOOTING FOR EFI ELECTRONIC CIRCUIT WITH VOLT/ OHMMETER."
    },
